WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.136
How many days did each order take to ship?

2
00:00:04.136 --> 00:00:06.360
DATEDIFF answers that for every row.

3
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.549
In this lesson, you will learn to write DATEDIFF in a calculated column, fix

4
00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:17.480
the errors caused by wrong columns or missing arguments, and check the result. Let's measure time!

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:24.740
In Table view, on the Orders table, I click New column, and name it Dated Interval.

6
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:31.987
I type DATEDIFF.

7
00:00:31.987 --> 00:00:33.436
It needs three things:

8
00:00:33.436 --> 00:00:37.420
the first date, the second date, and the interval, like DAY.

9
00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.807
I choose Orders, Date, then Orders, Shipped Date, then DAY.

10
00:00:44.807 --> 00:00:46.760
But the column shows errors.

11
00:00:58.900 --> 00:00:59.725
The message:

12
00:00:59.725 --> 00:01:03.851
cannot convert value July, of type text, to type date.

13
00:01:03.851 --> 00:01:08.390
The Date column holds month names as text, not real dates.

14
00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:10.040
DATEDIFF needs real dates.

15
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:32.339
When I rebuild the formula, a new message appears:

16
00:01:32.339 --> 00:01:34.249
too few arguments were passed.

17
00:01:34.249 --> 00:01:36.160
One argument is still missing.

18
00:01:58.900 --> 00:01:59.663
The fix:

19
00:01:59.663 --> 00:02:01.569
use the real date columns.

20
00:02:01.569 --> 00:02:05.000
Orders, Order Date, then Orders, Shipped Date, then DAY.

21
00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:10.966
Now the column fills with numbers:

22
00:02:10.966 --> 00:02:13.377
ten, twelve, seven days, and so on.

23
00:02:13.377 --> 00:02:16.820
Each row shows how long that order took to ship.

24
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.638
To check, I look at Order Date and Shipped Date for a few rows,

25
00:02:32.638 --> 00:02:34.240
and count the days. They match!

26
00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.420
DATEDIFF is perfect for delays, ages, and durations. Well done!

